WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.611
Here are the thirty-six rolls

2
00:00:05.704 --> 00:00:07.387
of two dice again.

3
00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:11.032
A double has chance six in thirty-six

4
00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:12.060
one sixth.

5
00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.807
Now a friend rolls, hides the dice

6
00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:19.690
and gives you a hint: the sum is eight.

7
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.734
What is the chance of a double now?

8
00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:24.120
Pause and decide.

9
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.923
The hint throws away every roll

10
00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:31.645
whose sum is not eight.

11
00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:33.510
Only five cells are left

12
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.020
and they are still equally likely.

13
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.439
Exactly one of them is a double

14
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:40.878
four and four.

15
00:00:40.878 --> 00:00:43.755
So the answer is one in five

16
00:00:43.755 --> 00:00:45.290
not one in six.

17
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.283
In symbols, the conditional probability of

18
00:00:50.394 --> 00:00:54.706
D given E is P of D and E, over P of E.

19
00:00:54.706 --> 00:00:59.349
One thirty-sixth over five thirty-sixths

20
00:00:59.349 --> 00:01:01.560
which is one fifth.

21
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.948
Dividing by P of E rescales

22
00:01:04.023 --> 00:01:05.532
the five cells left

23
00:01:05.608 --> 00:01:07.645
so that their probabilities

24
00:01:07.721 --> 00:01:09.230
add up to one again.

25
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.562
Now swap the roles.

26
00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:13.038
Given a double

27
00:01:13.038 --> 00:01:16.911
what is the chance that the sum is eight?

28
00:01:16.911 --> 00:01:19.770
Frame the six doubles instead.

29
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.008
Only four and four has sum eight.

30
00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:27.896
So P of E given D is one in six.

31
00:01:27.896 --> 00:01:33.080
One fifth and one sixth: different numbers.

32
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.691
Both use the same overlap

33
00:01:35.691 --> 00:01:37.777
the cell four and four.

34
00:01:37.777 --> 00:01:40.994
But they divide by different events.

35
00:01:40.994 --> 00:01:43.950
Always ask: which event is given?

36
00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.640
Sometimes a hint changes nothing.

37
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.562
Let R be: the red die is even.

38
00:01:50.562 --> 00:01:53.955
That is three rows, eighteen cells

39
00:01:53.955 --> 00:01:54.898
one half.

40
00:01:54.898 --> 00:01:58.573
Let T be: the sum is seven, six cells

41
00:01:58.573 --> 00:01:59.610
one sixth.

42
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.473
Given R, frame the even rows.

43
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:06.287
Three of the eighteen cells have sum seven

44
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:08.887
three eighteenths, one sixth.

45
00:02:08.887 --> 00:02:11.400
The same as before the hint.

46
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.681
Events like this are called independent.

47
00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:17.254
The definition

48
00:02:17.254 --> 00:02:21.283
P of A and B equals P of A times P of B.

49
00:02:21.381 --> 00:02:24.035
Here three thirty-sixths is

50
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:26.590
one half times one sixth.

51
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.199
When P of B is not zero

52
00:02:29.293 --> 00:02:31.928
this is the same as saying P

53
00:02:32.022 --> 00:02:34.564
of A given B equals P of A

54
00:02:34.564 --> 00:02:38.140
the frame does not change the chance.

55
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.661
Now keep R, the red die even

56
00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:45.211
but ask about E, the sum is eight.

57
00:02:45.211 --> 00:02:47.442
Inside the even rows

58
00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:51.600
three cells have sum eight: two and six

59
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.440
four and four, six and two.

60
00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.172
So P of E given R is one sixth

61
00:02:58.281 --> 00:03:00.899
but P of E alone is only

62
00:03:01.008 --> 00:03:03.517
five out of thirty-six.

63
00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:06.899
Knowing R makes E more likely

64
00:03:06.899 --> 00:03:10.390
so R and E are not independent.

65
00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.044
A warning about a tempting mistake.

66
00:03:14.044 --> 00:03:16.680
A double, and a sum of seven.

67
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.139
They never happen together

68
00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:21.598
no double adds up to seven.

69
00:03:21.598 --> 00:03:23.970
These events are disjoint.

70
00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.336
Disjoint sounds like independent

71
00:03:27.336 --> 00:03:29.529
but it is the opposite.

72
00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:33.458
P of both is zero, while P of D times P of

73
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.560
T is one thirty-sixth.

74
00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.835
If you learn that you rolled a double

75
00:03:38.835 --> 00:03:42.144
you know for certain the sum is not seven.

76
00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:45.377
Disjoint events with positive probability

77
00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:47.070
are always dependent.

78
00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:50.485
One more use of conditioning

79
00:03:50.485 --> 00:03:52.342
turning it around.

80
00:03:52.342 --> 00:03:54.981
One email in five is spam.

81
00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:58.109
The word prize appears in three

82
00:03:58.207 --> 00:04:00.456
out of ten spam emails

83
00:04:00.456 --> 00:04:04.170
and in one out of fifty other emails.

84
00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.821
Make a sample space with four outcomes.

85
00:04:07.821 --> 00:04:09.259
Spam with prize

86
00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:11.798
one fifth times three tenths

87
00:04:11.798 --> 00:04:13.237
three fiftieths.

88
00:04:13.237 --> 00:04:15.184
Other mail with prize

89
00:04:15.184 --> 00:04:17.892
four fifths times one fiftieth

90
00:04:17.892 --> 00:04:20.600
two over a hundred twenty-five.

91
00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.157
An email contains prize.

92
00:04:23.157 --> 00:04:24.904
Frame that column.

93
00:04:24.996 --> 00:04:27.387
Its total is nineteen over

94
00:04:27.479 --> 00:04:29.134
two hundred fifty.

95
00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:32.628
This is the law of total probability

96
00:04:32.628 --> 00:04:36.030
add the pieces with B and without B.

97
00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.463
So the chance of spam, given prize

98
00:04:39.551 --> 00:04:44.394
is three fiftieths over nineteen two-hundred-fiftieths

99
00:04:44.394 --> 00:04:46.244
fifteen nineteenths

100
00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:48.798
about zero point seven nine.

101
00:04:48.798 --> 00:04:51.000
That is Bayes's formula

102
00:04:51.088 --> 00:04:53.906
and it moved the chance from one

103
00:04:53.994 --> 00:04:56.460
fifth to almost four fifths.

104
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.324
Try it yourself

105
00:04:58.324 --> 00:05:01.132
one chip in two hundred is faulty

106
00:05:01.212 --> 00:05:04.903
and a test flags ninety-nine percent of faulty

107
00:05:04.983 --> 00:05:07.952
chips and three percent of good ones.

108
00:05:07.952 --> 00:05:10.760
Is a flagged chip probably faulty?

109
00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.071
Next lecture puts numbers on outcomes

110
00:05:14.071 --> 00:05:17.188
random variables, their expected value

111
00:05:17.188 --> 00:05:18.590
and their spread.
